# Secrets — NEVER commit (parity with other PWG / Creative Machines repos)
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.env
# ^ BROAD GLOB. It eats cuts/<tag>/cut.env, which is a CUT RECORD, not a
# secret: two pins the rollforward gate binds, and no credential ever. The
# negation below is deliberately narrow -- one filename under one directory --
# so nothing else escapes the rule above.
#
# This bit hard. The v1.0.24 cut.env was written, staged, and silently dropped;
# the gate passed locally because the file was on DISK, and would have failed
# in CI where the checkout has only what git tracks. A green measured against
# a working tree is not a green.
#
# ANY credential belongs in a *.env that this negation does NOT cover.
!cuts/*/cut.env
SERVER_INFO.md
config.local.*
*.pem
*.key
*_private*
*credentials*
# This one bit on 2026-08-10 too: bin/require_signing_credentials.sh, a guard
# script holding no secret at all, was silently skipped by `git add -A`. The
# commit pushed without it and cut.yml was left calling a file absent from the
# repo. Caught only because its test asserts the script exists. Renamed to
# bin/require_signing_secrets.sh rather than adding a second negation below --
# for a file we own, a rename is cheaper than an exception to a security rule.
# If you add a script whose name contains any word on this list, check
# `git check-ignore -v <path>` BEFORE you commit.
*token*
# Vendored Doctor source module (NOT a secret) - the broad *token* guard above
# otherwise swallows it, which dropped it from the vendor and blanked the iOS
# Pairing tab (chat_token.py is lazy-imported by web_ui.py). Keep tracked.
!vendor/doctor/agent/chat_token.py
# ...and the RULE, not a third exception. The line above was added after
# `chat_token.py` was swallowed and blanked the iOS Pairing tab. It fixed that
# ONE file. On 2026-09-02 the identical thing happened to
# `vendor/doctor/agent/extension_token.py`: written to disk, silently untracked,
# and the shipped Doctor imported a module the commit did not contain. A2 caught
# it by reading the vendored tree; `git status` never said a word.
#
# Two instances of one shape means the exception was the wrong fix. A SOURCE
# FILE IS NOT A CREDENTIAL. The globs still cover what they were written for --
# an extensionless secret, a .txt, a .json, a .pem -- and nothing below
# re-admits those. A credential hardcoded INSIDE a .py was never stopped by
# this file anyway; that is what the PII and secret scanners are for.
!*token*.py
!*token*.sh
!*token*.swift
!*token*.md
!*secret*.py
!*secret*.sh
!*secret*.swift
!*secret*.md
!*credentials*.py
!*credentials*.sh
!*credentials*.swift
!*credentials*.md
secrets/
credentials/

# State files (contain user-specific data)
state.json
*.state

# Real-person fixtures / private test directories (per HR015 Rule Zero)
fixtures_private/
conversations/
transcripts/
raw_data/
.pii-blocklist

# Python (if any test harnesses land here)
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.venv/
venv/
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/

# W8 / F6: the signed Safari extension .app.zip is a binary build artefact
# produced by CM020's bin/build-safari-extension.sh and staged here by ORM at
# cut time. It is large + signed-per-cut, so it is NOT committed to git; the
# vendor/extensions/ directory is kept tracked via its .gitkeep so the path
# exists for the staging step.
vendor/extensions/OstlerSafariExtension.app.zip

# IDE
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Logs
*.log
logs/

# User-specific deployment config
deploy.local.yml

# Operator session prompts
AGENT_PROMPT.md
REVIEW_FIXES_PROMPT.md

# Claude Code
.claude/

# Test artefacts
test_installs/
scratch/

# Xcode (gui/) – keep project.yml + sources, drop build output and
# user-specific Xcode metadata. The .xcodeproj is regenerated from
# project.yml via `xcodegen generate` so it doesn't need to live in
# git either, but we DO commit it for now so a fresh checkout can
# `xcodebuild ... build` without needing xcodegen.
gui/build/
gui/dist/
gui/DerivedData/
gui/**/xcuserdata/
gui/**/*.xcworkspace/xcuserdata/
gui/**/.build/
gui/**/Packages/

# Locally-built daemon binary bundled into the DMG at cut time. NOT tracked
# in source -- built fresh from the ostler-assistant source repo via
# `cargo build --release --bin zeroclaw` + signed + stripped, then copied
# to assistant-agent/bin/ostler-assistant by the operator-facing
# `make ship` runner. The customer-facing GitHub release tarball is the
# durable distribution channel; this bundling is the offline-capable
# fallback that ships in DMG Resources. CX-79b (2026-05-25).
assistant-agent/bin/
# CX-88 (DMG #48g, 2026-05-29): make stage-daemon fetches the v0.4.4 daemon
# tarball from the private ostler-ai/ostler-assistant release and extracts
# the .app bundle here. Bundle phase in gui/project.yml copies it into
# OstlerInstaller.app/Contents/Resources/assistant-agent/OstlerAssistant.app/
# at xcodebuild time. NOT tracked in source -- the tarball is recreated
# on every `make ship` run.
assistant-agent/OstlerAssistant.app/

# F1 hygiene: never bundle stale python-build output (pre-F1 consent_cli)
vendor/ostler_security/build/
**/*.egg-info/

# tests/test_wiki_tailnet_gate.sh scratch (repo-local so Docker Desktop can bind-mount it)
.wiki-gate-test.*

# scratch dir for tests/test_pinned_wiki_image_has_design_system.sh
.wikicheck.*/

# Scratch files the orphan gate writes when its expired-refs tempfile could not
# be created. With EXPIRED_REFS empty, "${EXPIRED_REFS}.sorted" resolves to a
# BARE ".sorted" IN THE CURRENT WORKING DIRECTORY -- so a gate run on a GNU host
# littered the repo root with .sorted/.new/.new.base/.gone, and one of my own
# mutation runs committed 852 lines of them by accident. The tempfile is now
# portable and hard-fails, so these should never appear again; ignored so that
# if they ever do, they cannot ride into a commit unnoticed.
.sorted
.new
.new.base
.gone

# The bundled CPython that scripts/ttywalk.sh REQUIRES in the repo root.
#
# ttywalk refuses to walk without it, and says why: install.sh takes the bundled
# interpreter when python/bin/python3.11 is executable and falls back to a
# brew python when it is not, so a walk without it runs the whole install on an
# interpreter no customer ever uses. The harness therefore tells you to copy it
# out of the DMG you are walking:
#
#     cp -R "/Volumes/Install Ostler/OstlerInstaller.app/Contents/Resources/python" ./python
#
# That instruction puts ~70MB of build product at the repo root of a PUBLIC repo,
# and nothing ignored it. `git add -A` in a walk checkout would have committed a
# signed interpreter. It has never happened -- `git log --all -- python/bin/python3.11`
# is empty -- which is luck, not a guard, and the same luck the .sorted/.new
# entries below were added after running out of.
python/
